# The content list — Miclone design.md §9's load step.
#
# **One list, and this is it.** Everything that needs the game — the server and
# every probe that generates terrain — calls `load_mods` and reads the
# registries off the `Game` it gets back. The browser client does not: it is
# handed the registries over the wire (server-selected content).
#
# ## The content is compiled in
#
# `mods/default` is imported like any other module and its `register_all` is
# called. That is the Commons' loading model — a selected module list and
# normal Gene loading (docs/proposals/world.md §13.1). Miclone also had a
# runtime loader that read `mods/` off disk into a sandbox with manifest
# `^grants`; the Commons dropped it, because the proposal asks for neither
# grants (§1.2) nor the mod framework as a prerequisite (§1.4). Content here is
# trusted code in this package.
#
# `^profile web` keeps the module portable, so the web-profile specs and
# `tools/mesh_bench.mjs`/`tools/world_build.mjs` generate the same game the
# server does.

(mod mods ^profile web)

(import [Game new_game] ^from "./api.gene")
(import [register_all] ^from "../mods/default/src/default.gene")

# The game, registered. Called once per process, before anything reads a
# content id — which is every caller's first line, because §2 assigns ids at
# load and nothing means anything before that.
#
# Mod order is the list's order, and with one mod there is nothing to say about
# it. §9's `depends`/`optional_depends` ordering arrives with the second mod and
# with the manifest that declares the dependency; a topological sort over one
# node would be a sort nobody could get wrong and nobody could check.
(fn load_mods [] : Game
  (var game (new_game))
  (register_all game)
  game)
